 "orphan_driver_buttons": [
  "SEG01 0x02 \"RHYTHM ORGANIST\" -- no CPL silk cell (candidate landing spot for BIG BAND & SWING)",
  "SEG02 0x40 \"RHYTHM 60s & 70s\" -- no CPL silk cell (candidate landing spot for GOSPEL & BLUES)",
  "SEG06 0x80 \"CUSTOM PANEL (guess)\" -- guesses at CPL SEG6/SW7, which is an UNPOPULATED matrix slot (diode D1188, no switch); no physical CPL button",
  "SEG0D 0x40 \"DISK MENU\" -- no populated CPR cell (extra event, likely other-board/duplicate)",
  "SEG11 0x01 \"ACCOMP 2 ON\" -- no populated CPR cell (mixer/part-toggle event, not a CPR switch)",
  "SEG11 0x10 \"RIGHT 1 ON\" -- no populated CPR cell (part-toggle; alt candidate for a PART SELECT press)",
  "SEG11 0x20 \"RIGHT 2 ON\" -- no populated CPR cell (part-toggle; alt candidate for a PART SELECT press)",
  "SEG12 0x01 \"ACCOMP 1 ON\" -- no populated CPR cell (mixer/part-toggle event)",
  "SEG13 0x01 \"LEFT ON\" -- no populated CPR cell (part-toggle; alt candidate for a PART SELECT press)",
  "SEG18 0x01 \"Fn 1009 (CPC)\" -- valuator/system placeholder (wire ADDR 0xD2), not a board switch cell",
  "SEG20 0x01 \"Fn 1020\" -- system placeholder (wire ADDR 0x17), not a board switch cell",
  "SEG16/SEG17/SEG19/SEG1A -- VALUATOR wire ports (DATA dial / pitchbend / TEMPO-PROGRAM encoder, ADDR 0xD0/0xD1/0xD3/0x10); analog inputs to the sub-CPUs, not SEGxSW switch cells (map to driver DIAL / TEMPO_KNOB, not a button bit)",
  "SEG1B/SEG1C/SEG1E/SEG1F -- no-wire IPT_UNUSED placeholder ports (no panel-serial path)",
  "SEG1D -- no-wire placeholder = the descriptor slot (normSeg 0x1D) that the CPSD transport switches normalize onto; the actual buttons live on the driver SDSW port (GPIO 0x9CC00008), not here",
  "DIAL / TEMPO_KNOB / VOL_MAIN / VOL_APCSEQ / VOL_MIC / VOL_LINEIN / REARSW -- analog/config controls (CPR rotary encoder + CPL ADC pots + JACK-board rear switch), not part of any SEGxSW switch matrix"
 ],
 "concerns": "SCRAMBLE STATUS PER BOARD (drives how much to trust each bit): CPL and CPC bridge by POSITION -- physical SEGn -> wire ADDR 0xC0+n -> normSeg n -> driver SEGn, and DATA bit = 1<<SW, so within CPL_SEGn/CPC_SEGn the port bit ALREADY equals the driver mask for SEG00/03/04/05/06/07/08/09/0A/0B (identity, confirmed cell-for-cell). CPR is a FULL firmware scramble: within a CPR_SEGn port the bit_mask (1<<SW) does NOT equal the driver mask -- each physical column repacks buttons across several driver normSegs (e.g. CPR_SEG0 spans driver SEG0C/0D/0E/15). Every CPR bridge is by function/label, never by position, and I have preserved the driver_seg/driver_mask so the rename keeps each button firing the same event.\n\nCPL genre columns (CPL_SEG1/CPL_SEG2) are the weakest area: the driver's SEG01/SEG02 genre bit-order came from event-arg probing that notes/panel-matrix-service-manual.md flagged as CONTAMINATED by mode-gating, so I bridged by genre NAME (crossing between driver SEG01 and SEG02). Exact-name matches (MEMORY, CUSTOM, ENTERTAINER, MOVIE&SHOW, MARCH, LATIN&WORLD, BALLROOM, COUNTRY, MODERN DANCE, ROCK&POP, BALLAD) are solid; approximate ones -- SOUL & FUNK~\"SOUL & R&B\" (SEG02 0x04), JAZZ COMBO~\"JAZZ & SWING\" (SEG02 0x02), 8 & 16 BEAT (SEG02 0x80, silk also readable as \"R & B\") -- are medium/low and a live per-bit probe on the RHYTHM screen would confirm the order. BIG BAND & SWING and GOSPEL & BLUES have no driver bit; the two orphan driver genres (ORGANIST, 60s&70s) are their natural home but there is no name basis to assert it -- left UNBRIDGED both ways.\n\nDRIVER NAMES THAT SHOULD BE RENAMED (wire binding is correct, PORT_NAME is wrong): CPL_SEG0 LCDL 1-5 soft-keys currently sit on driver part-OFF names (ACCOMP 1/2 OFF, RIGHT 1/2 OFF, LEFT OFF) -- low confidence, the LCDL-to-part mapping is not clean. CPL_SEG7/SW1 AUTO MODE = driver \"APC / CHORD FINDER\"; CPL_SEG7/SW2 SOUND SET = driver \"SOUND ARRANGER\". CPC OTHER PARTS/TG (SEG05 0x01) = driver \"DRUM 1 OFF\" (KN5000 folklore); CPC EXIT (SEG0B 0x80) = driver \"ACCOMP 5 ON\". Renaming these is safe -- only the label changes.\n\nMUTE MATRIX caveat: CPC MUTE UP/DOWN N bridge to driver PART N ON/OFF (up=ON/even bit, down=OFF/odd bit); the wire binding is identity-solid, but notes/panel-matrix-service-manual.md found the firmware PART INDEX each mute actually drives is not a clean 1..16 order, so the driver's PART-NUMBER labeling (not the bind) may need firmware re-verification.\n\nCPR AMBIGUOUS GROUPS (LOW): the three PART SELECT cells (LEFT/RIGHT1/RIGHT2) and three CONDUCTOR cells (LEFT/RIGHT1/RIGHT2) map onto the driver's three undifferentiated PART SELECT bits (SEG0D 0x02, SEG0E 0x01/0x02) and three CONDUCTOR bits (SEG0F/SEG10/SEG11 0x02); the specific L/R1/R2->bit assignment shown is ARBITRARY. PART SELECT presses may instead fire the part-toggle orphans (LEFT ON SEG13 0x01 / RIGHT 1 ON SEG11 0x10 / RIGHT 2 ON SEG11 0x20). TRANSPOSE: R1(+/-) -> driver TRANSPOSE -/+ (medium); R2(+/-) -> driver R1/R2 OCTAVE -/+ (LOW -- the R2-rocker=OCTAVE-rocker equivalence and the +/- polarity within each pair are unverified). SOUND GROUP 1-8 -> PANEL MEMORY 1-8 bridged by NUMBER (small permutation risk); SOUND SET -> PANEL MEMORY SET; BANK VIEW -> PANEL MEMORY BANK SELECT.\n\nCPSD IS NOT A SCAN MATRIX: the SD panel has no sub-CPU and no SEGxSW matrix -- six discrete tactile switches read as a main-CPU GPIO byte (0x9CC00008, active-low, bits 0-5). The CPSD_SDSW port above is a REPURPOSING to fit the schema: seg_col=29 is the firmware descriptor segment 0x1D these switches normalize onto, and sw = the GPIO/descriptor bit (= event 0x20B5..0x20BA). These are already correctly bound in the driver to the dedicated SDSW port (masks 0x01-0x20), NOT to any SEGxx port; the driver's SEG1D placeholder is deliberately empty. The SD-slot cover detect SW921 is on the separate SD I/F block -> driver SDCOVER config (open => ERROR 93), not a matrix cell.\n\nDRIVER SEG NAME != physical column: because CPR is scrambled, a future rename must NOT simply relabel driver SEGnn ports to CP*_SEGn -- CPR_SEGn is a NEW port whose bits gather buttons from multiple driver normSegs. The cleanest implementation keeps the physical CP{board}_SEG{col} ports as the user-facing input, each bit forwarding to the existing normSeg/wire path (driver_seg/driver_mask) so panel_scan/seg_to_addr still emit the same [ADDR,DATA] frame."
